---
import ConceptLayout from '../../layouts/ConceptLayout.astro'
---
<ConceptLayout
	slug="tenancy"
	source={[
		'apps/web/src/db/schema/_helpers.ts',
		'apps/web/src/permissions/',
		'apps/web/src/api/routes/admin.ts',
		'apps/web/src/api/middleware/auth.ts',
		'apps/web/src/api/services/access.ts',
	]}
>
	<h2>One schema, one or many tenants</h2>
	<p>
		Every row of domain data belongs to a tenant, and the schema is identical whether the app runs
		for a single organisation or a thousand. <code>TENANCY_MODE</code> is configuration, not a fork
		of the codebase:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>multi</strong> — users belong to many organisations through <code>tenant_users</code>;
			the session carries the current one, an org switcher moves between them, and global admins
			manage every tenant from the admin area.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>single</strong> — one organisation is created at bootstrap and every admitted user is
			auto-joined to it. The switcher and the tenant-select screen disappear from the UI and answer
			404 on the server. Members, roles, invitations and settings all stay.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<div class="why">
		<p>
			<strong>Why one schema.</strong> The alternative — a "simple" single-tenant build you later
			retrofit — means a migration and an audit of every query on the day the second customer
			arrives. Here, flipping to <code>multi</code> later needs no migration at all, because the
			tenant column was never optional.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>Sign-up is a mode too</h2>
	<p>
		<code>SIGNUP_MODE</code> decides what happens when somebody who was never invited signs in.
		Invite-only is the default.
	</p>
	<table>
		<thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>An uninvited login gets…</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td><code>invite_only</code></td><td>a user row and a "pending" screen. No organisation is created, and there is nothing for an admin to review.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>approval</code></td><td>the same, plus one access request that a global admin approves into a new or existing organisation, or rejects with a note.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>open</code></td><td>a personal organisation of their own.</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		The access request is written when the email is <em>verified</em>, not when a magic link is
		requested — so the queue cannot be filled by typing a stranger's address into the login box. An
		optional domain allow-list turns away obvious outsiders before any of that.
	</p>
	<p>
		Invited users behave the same in all three modes: the pending-invitation handler runs first on
		every login path, and every fallback gates on "has no memberships" rather than "is a new user" —
		which is what stops someone who just lost their last organisation from being stranded.
	</p>

	<h2>Roles and abilities</h2>
	<p>
		A membership carries one of <code>owner</code>, <code>admin</code> or <code>member</code>. A
		fourth role, <code>support</code>, can only be minted from the admin area, is excluded from
		member counts, and is deliberately visible to the customer. Separately,
		<code>users.isGlobalAdmin</code> is a platform flag — not a role inside any organisation.
	</p>
	<p>
		Permissions are CASL abilities computed on the server and shipped to the browser with the
		session, so a nav item, a page guard and the route behind them all consult the same rules. As a
		rough shape: owners and admins manage their organisation; members read it and write the things
		they own; support behaves like an admin; global admins can do anything.
	</p>
	<div class="note">
		<p>
			Two actions ignore the ability system on purpose and check the role directly: deleting an
			organisation, and changing who owns it. Those are one-way doors, and an explicit
			<code>role === 'owner'</code> test is easier to audit than a rule buried in a policy.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>Groups, and who can see what</h2>
	<p>
		A role says what kind of thing you may do. A group says which rows you may see. An organisation
		declares group <em>types</em> — Department, Region, Client — each holding groups, each holding
		members; that membership joins the authenticated context and decides who may read a knowledge
		document or a dashboard.
	</p>
	<p>
		It is part of the core rather than an optional extra, and it is inert until somebody uses it:
		every row ships visible to the whole organisation, so an app that never creates a group type
		behaves exactly as it did before one existed.
	</p>

	<div class="why">
		<p>
			<strong>Why being restricted is a column.</strong> Restriction is an explicit value on the
			row, not "has rows in the grants table". Infer it from the grants instead, and deleting the last group
			a document was shared with publishes that document to the entire organisation, silently. Here
			the row stays restricted with an empty grant list, which matches nobody — so it narrows to its
			owner and to admins. Deleting a group that still grants something is refused with a count of
			what it would affect, and forcing it still only ever narrows.
		</p>
	</div>

	<p>
		The check itself is SQL, written once: a predicate <em>added to</em> the tenant predicate, never
		a replacement for it, and never a condition buried in the ability system — the same rule "is
		this row mine?" has always followed here. Owners, admins and support are not narrowed. A row you
		may not see answers the same 404 as one that does not exist, so the API is not an existence
		oracle in either direction.
	</p>
	<p>
		Every read path follows it: the documents list, the hybrid search, the three tools an agent uses
		to read the knowledge base, the answers the chat box gives, the uploaded original behind a
		document, and the dashboards. An agent run resolves its requester's groups when it runs rather
		than when it was queued, so a run started before somebody left a group does not read on their
		old access. Being in a group grants read only — editing a document or a dashboard stays with its
		owner or an admin.
	</p>

	<div class="note">
		<p>
			A membership change is a nudge to the people it moved, so somebody who loses a group watches
			the content disappear rather than clicking into a 404. There is no directory sync yet: SCIM
			groups and SAML group claims are the obvious next step, and the kit speaks neither protocol.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>Isolation is a predicate you cannot forget quietly</h2>
	<p>
		Every domain query filters by the tenant id taken from the authenticated context — never from
		anything the client sent. That predicate is also what keeps the composite indexes selective, so
		it is load-bearing twice over.
	</p>
	<p>
		Cross-tenant SQL exists in exactly two places: the admin routes, and the pre-tenant part of the
		auth path (sign-in, invite acceptance). A test pins that list, so widening it is a decision
		somebody makes on purpose rather than a refactor that slips through.
	</p>
	<p>
		On top of that, every tenant table also carries a Postgres row-level-security policy. The
		policies ship switched off, with the application role created but unable to log in, and a
		catalog-driven test that fails the build if a new tenant table arrives without one. Turning
		enforcement on is a configuration change — the scaffolding is already in place and already
		tested.
	</p>

	<h2>The admin area, and "entering" a customer</h2>
	<p>
		Everything cross-tenant lives behind one global-admin middleware, so the blast radius of that
		capability is one file. When an admin enters a customer's organisation to help them, the app
		inserts a real <code>support</code> membership row rather than special-casing the request. The
		auth middleware keeps its single invariant — you must be a member — and the customer can see who
		is in their organisation.
	</p>

	<h2>The CLI is inside a tenant too</h2>
	<p>
		Logging the CLI in ends with a tenant-scoped API key, so every command already runs inside one
		organisation and travels the same middleware and the same abilities as the UI. There is no
		second permission model for machines.
	</p>
</ConceptLayout>
